w possible (T4 gone), no pairing (only 1), no pung possible (only 2 unshown T2 left).  \nW3 \u2014 only useful for W3,W4,W5 chow with scarce tiles.  \nB6 \u2014 extra B6 could be used in B4,B5,B6 chow, but we could also discard B6 and keep B4 for future chow if we get B5,B6 from draws. But discarding B6 reduces chance for B4,B5,B6 chow.  \n\nBut if we discard B6, we keep B4, we need both B5 and B6 again \u2014 harder.  \n\nBetter discard: T2 or W3.  \n\nLet's check unshown: W3 has 3 left \u2014 maybe pair possible. W6 has 4 left \u2014 maybe pair or chow.  \n\nMost useless: T2 (no chow, no pair likely).  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:** The best discard now is T2 because it has little potential for chow/pung given T4 is depleted, and keeping other tiles gives more flexibility for chows/pungs.  \n\n**Reason:** T2 cannot form a chow since T4 is exhausted (0 left). It has low pairing chance (only 2 left unseen). Keeping W3, B4, B6, W6, J1, T6 gives better options for chows/pungs with available tiles.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T2"}
